 ## Overview
-Octree-based isosurfacing replaces the uniform Cartesian grid with an adaptive octree spatial discretization for surface extraction algorithms such as Marching Cubes and Dual Contouring [S1]. This approach enables variable resolution, refining only where the surface geometry demands it, but it sacrifices the topological guarantees of uniform grids, potentially introducing cracks in the resulting mesh if the octree is not sufficiently adapted to the surface [S1].
+Octree-based isosurfacing replaces the uniform Cartesian grid with an octree spatial subdivision to discretize the domain for surface extraction algorithms like Marching Cubes or Dual Contouring [S1]. This approach enables adaptive resolution, refining the mesh density only where necessary, but it sacrifices the topological guarantees of uniform grids, potentially causing cracks in the resulting surface if the octree is not sufficiently adapted to the geometry [S1].
 
